---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import "../../../styles/story.css";
import ReadingModes from "../../../components/ReadingModes.astro";
import Cite from "../../../components/Cite.astro";
import DecisionTimeline from "../../../components/story/DecisionTimeline.astro";
import StorySources from "../../../components/StorySources.astro";
import StoryState from "../../../components/StoryState.astro";
import StateEvent from "../../../components/StateEvent.astro";
import { publishedState, eventPath } from "../../../lib/state.mjs";
import { claim } from "../../../lib/claims.mjs";
import { event, computed, versions, timeline } from "../../../data/fraud-tip-program-2026-10-20-trump-launches-25-million-fraud-tip-program.mjs";
const story = "fraud-tip-program--2026-10-20-trump-launches-25-million-fraud-tip-program";
const short = (id) => claim(story, id).short;
const timelineEntries = timeline.map((t) => ({ date: t.date, title: short(t.c), current: t.current, cites: [{ c: t.c }] }));
const state = publishedState().events.get("event-fraud-tip-program-2026-10-20-trump-launches-25-million-fraud-tip-program");
if (!state) throw new Error("Missing publication selection: event-fraud-tip-program-2026-10-20-trump-launches-25-million-fraud-tip-program");
if (state.status === "merged") return Astro.redirect(eventPath(state.survivor), 301);
---
{state.status === "retired" ? <StateEvent view={state} /> : <BaseLayout title={`${event.title} | The Catch`} current="Events">
  <div slot="locator" class="locator"><a href="/events/">Series</a><a href="/events/fraud-tip-program/">Fraud tip program</a><span>October 20, 2026</span></div>
  <div class="content">
    <main class="story" id="story-root">
      <p class="story-kicker" data-layer="fact">Fraud tip program &middot; October 20, 2026 &middot; updated {event.updated}</p>
      <h1 data-layer="fact">{event.title}</h1>
      <p class="story-dek" data-layer="fact">{event.dek}<Cite c="treasury-25m" /><Cite c="covered-fraud" /><Cite c="terms-charges-defined" /><Cite c="terms-sole-discretion" /></p>
      <ReadingModes />
      <nav class="story-toc" aria-label="In this story" data-layer="fact">
        <details class="toc-disclosure" open><summary><span>In this story</span><span class="toc-current" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#what-happened">What happened</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-rules">What the written rules say</a></li>
          <li><a href="#why-now">Why a new program</a></li>
          <li><a href="#shirley">Shirley's $10,000</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-sides">The two sides</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-catch">The Catch</a></li>
          <li><a href="#next">What happens next</a></li>
          <li><a href="#records">The records</a></li>
        </ol>
        </details>
      </nav>

      <section id="what-happened">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">On October 20 the Justice Department announced the Fraud Whistleblower Incentive Program, which offers a person whose tip leads to criminal charges an award of $10,000 to $30,000, if the department chooses to pay.<Cite c="doj-launched-whip" /><Cite c="terms-amount-at-charging" /> It covers health care fraud and fraud on federal benefit programs, with hospice, home health care, day care and special services first, and the Treasury Department committed $25 million to it.<Cite c="covered-fraud" /><Cite c="treasury-25m" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">President Trump announced it in the Oval Office with Vice President JD Vance, who chairs the administration's Task Force to Eliminate Fraud.<Cite c="task-force-vance" /> Trump described the payments differently from the department. Asked whether it was 1 percent up to a $30,000 limit, he said: "It's 1 percent. There's no limit." He said "it's not a criminal thing," that a tipster is paid "if we recover the money," and that he would be "the arbitrator of all of these decisions."<Cite c="trump-1pct-no-limit" /><Cite c="trump-no-charges" /><Cite c="trump-arbitrator" /> A White House official said Nick Shirley, who posted a video about Minneapolis day care centers on December 26, 2025, would get $10,000, and Vance said Shirley "is going to get a financial reward" for uncovering fraud.<Cite c="shirley-10k-announced" /><Cite c="shirley-video-date" /><Cite c="vance-shirley-reward" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Tips go through america.gov/fraud, which says reports go straight to the Treasury Department, and the Justice Department decides awards in its sole discretion, with no appeal to a court.<Cite c="tips-via-america-gov" /><Cite c="terms-sole-discretion" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="the-rules">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What the written rules say</h2>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>How much, when and who decides: Trump's account and the Justice Department's terms</strong></figcaption>
          <div class="data-table-wrap">
            <table class="data-table">
              <thead><tr><th></th><th>What Trump said on October 20</th><th>What the written terms say</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                {versions.map((v) => (
                  <tr><th scope="row">{v.point}</th><td>{short(v.trump)}<Cite c={v.trump} /></td><td>{short(v.terms)}<Cite c={v.terms} /></td></tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </figure>
        <p data-layer="narrative">On one point the written rules are looser than Trump's version. The department's FAQ says a tip can earn an award even when the scheme failed or caused little or no loss, as long as it leads to charges.<Cite c="faq-no-recovery-needed" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Could a tipster be paid 1 percent with no limit? Not under the reward law the terms cite, which caps any one reward at ${computed.capMillions} million unless the reward is for terrorism or another statute authorizes more.<Cite c="law-cap-3m" /> Trump's own example was a tipster who finds $1 billion in fraud; 1 percent of that is ${computed.onePctOfBillionMillions} million, more than the cap, so such an award would need a different statute, and it is two-fifths of the ${computed.poolMillions} million Treasury committed.<Cite c="trump-billion-example" /><Cite c="one-pct-billion-over-cap" /><Cite c="one-pct-of-billion-vs-pool" /> The same law gives a president the one written role in rewards like these: one of $250,000 or more needs the personal approval of the attorney general or the president, far above the {computed.maxAward} these terms allow.<Cite c="law-250k-approval" /><Cite c="terms-amount-at-charging" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Others in the administration gave versions of their own. Before the event, a White House official said tipsters could get up to $30,000 if their information leads to a successful fraud conviction or a significant federal enforcement action.<Cite c="wh-official-preview" /> On stage, Vance said "our plan is not to limit this just to criminal prosecutions," and the next day his office posted that tipsters would get a commission if their information leads to a successful fraud conviction.<Cite c="vance-not-only-prosecutions" /><Cite c="vance-post-conviction" /> The reporting site says a tipster may be eligible if the report leads to criminal charges or to recovering stolen money, while Treasury's own post that evening repeated the department's $10,000 to $30,000 for tips that lead to criminal charges.<Cite c="portal-charges-or-recovery" /><Cite c="treasury-repeats-range" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="why-now">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Why a new program</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The program came {computed.daysDirectiveToLaunch} days after an order from inside the department. On October 1, Colin McDonald, head of the new National Fraud Enforcement Division, told his staff to design programs that give whistleblowers a reason to bring forward information on fraud.<Cite c="directive-oct1" /> The department's case for this one is speed: it says most government whistleblower programs pay only after a criminal case is fully resolved, and this one pays earlier.<Cite c="doj-reason-early-pay" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">An older route already pays a share of the money. The False Claims Act reaches anyone who knowingly submits false claims to the government. A whistleblower who brings a successful lawsuit under it generally receives 15 to 30 percent of the recovery, with smaller or no awards in some cases the law sets out.<Cite c="fca-covers-false-claims" /><Cite c="fca-share" /> The March 16 executive order that created Vance's task force told the attorney general to promote those suits over fraud in federal benefit programs.<Cite c="task-force-vance" /><Cite c="eo-promote-fca" /> Vance said over 99 percent of the fraud the administration is finding is not covered by the False Claims Act.<Cite c="vance-99pct" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The new terms say a tipster can also collect from other government whistleblower programs later in a case.<Cite c="stackable" /> The older Justice Department program they name says, in rules revised in July, that a person who gets an award for substantially the same misconduct from another government program is not eligible, and it pays only on forfeitures above $1 million.<Cite c="cwa-excludes-double" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="shirley">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Shirley's $10,000</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">What was Shirley paid for, and under which rules? The White House credited his reporting with helping bring a surge of federal resources into Minnesota, followed by investigations, arrests and new charges.<Cite c="wh-credits-shirley" /> Shirley's own site says he walked out of the Oval Office with a $10,000 award, which it says the White House linked to charges after his video. The Star Tribune reported that no check was given to him in front of journalists.<Cite c="shirley-site-received" /><Cite c="check-not-shown" /></p>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>The Minnesota charges, the video and the program</strong></figcaption>
          <DecisionTimeline entries={timelineEntries} heading="In order" />
        </figure>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Federal prosecutors had been bringing charges in the Feeding Our Future case since 2022, and a February 2024 charge against another defendant already named the Future Leaders Early Learning Center, one of the centers in the video.<Cite c="fof-charges-since-2022" /><Cite c="abdi-2024-names-center" /><Cite c="mahamud-charged-feb" /> Fahima Mahamud, its owner, was first charged in February 2026 and pleaded guilty to wire fraud and conspiracy on July 9, three months before the program was announced.<Cite c="mahamud-charged-feb" /><Cite c="mahamud-plea-july" /><Cite c="doj-launched-whip" /> The Justice Department's release, terms and FAQ do not mention Shirley or his payment, and the White House did not explain how he was chosen or how the $10,000 was set.<Cite c="doj-silent-on-shirley" /><Cite c="shirley-unexplained" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Trump said reporters could take part if their outlets approved.<Cite c="trump-reporters" /> Under the terms, information found only in the news does not count as original unless the tipster was its source.<Cite c="terms-news-not-original" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="the-sides">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">The two sides</h2>
        <h3 data-layer="fact">The administration</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">White House spokeswoman Lauren Bis said the administration has zero tolerance for people who steal from Americans.<Cite c="wh-zero-tolerance" /> The terms answer the risk of false tips: the department can permanently bar people who send frivolous or fraudulent tips, and false statements can be prosecuted.<Cite c="terms-false-tips" /></p>
        <h3 data-layer="fact">The critics</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Joe Spielberger of the Project on Government Oversight said the program "only incentivizes political provocateurs to target anyone the administration deems an enemy."<Cite c="pogo-critique" /> Mark Greenblatt, a former Interior Department inspector general, said putting discretion over federal dollars in one person's hands invites problems, and David Rosenfeld, a former SEC enforcement official, said he had never heard of a program where awards are up to the president.<Cite c="greenblatt-critique" /><Cite c="rosenfeld-critique" /></p>
        <h3 data-layer="fact">The people a tip names, and the tipster</h3>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The terms protect the tipster's identity and say nothing about the people a tip names.<Cite c="terms-tipster-confidential" /> Treasury says it will review, sort, de-duplicate and refer tips to the agency, inspector general or law enforcement office it picks. From November 5, its investigatory tip records are exempt from Privacy Act rules that would let the people named in them see or correct them.<Cite c="treasury-routes-tips" /><Cite c="privacy-rule-nov5" /> The tipster's protection has limits: the terms let a tipster's identity come out in court or go to other agencies, require cooperation that can include testifying, and pay no one whose identity is not verified.<Cite c="tipster-exposure" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="the-catch">
        <aside class="the-catch" data-layer="fact">
          <p class="tt-label">The Catch</p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <h3>The 1 percent is Trump's, not the program's<Cite c="trump-1pct-no-limit" /><Cite c="terms-amount-at-charging" /></h3>
              <p data-layer="narrative">The Hill reported that the program "will offer a 1 percent commission to those who provide a tip that leads to a successful conviction," and Spectrum News that it "would reward whistleblowers with a 1% bounty for helping the federal government recover the money."<Cite c="hill-1pct" /><Cite c="spectrum-1pct" /> Deseret News wrote that it "will allow whistleblowers in some cases to receive 1% of the funds recouped when fraud is proved in court."<Cite c="deseret-1pct" /> Each gave Trump's Oval Office account as the program's terms. The written terms offer $10,000 to $30,000 per tip, if the department chooses to pay.<Cite c="trump-1pct-no-limit" /><Cite c="terms-amount-at-charging" /></p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Smaller: the award does not wait for a conviction<Cite c="terms-amount-at-charging" /></h3>
              <p data-layer="narrative">The Hill's line and the Guardian's description of a program that "awards compensation to those who submit tips resulting in conviction or significant federal enforcement" both set the bar at a conviction.<Cite c="hill-1pct" /><Cite c="guardian-conviction" /> That was the White House official's account before the event.<Cite c="wh-official-preview" /> The terms count a tip that leads to criminal charges and pay at or around the time the charges are filed.<Cite c="terms-charges-defined" /><Cite c="terms-amount-at-charging" /></p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="next">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happens next</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The program is a pilot with an initial three-year term and could be expanded to other kinds of fraud.<Cite c="pilot-three-years" /> Its terms set each award at $10,000 to $30,000 per tip; Trump's 1 percent would take new terms, and any award above ${computed.capMillions} million a statute other than the reward law they cite.<Cite c="terms-amount-at-charging" /><Cite c="law-cap-3m" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="records">
      <StorySources event={event.slug} />
      <StoryState view={state} supporting />
      </section>
    </main>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>}
